</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-double-integrals-rectangles">
<title>Double Integrals Over Rectangles</title>

<p>
As we will see, double integrals can be interpreted geometrically as
the volume under a surface <m>z = f(x,y)</m>. As an example, consider
the surface <m>z = x^2 + y + 5</m>, which is shown in
<xref ref="fig-volume-one-box-tikz"/>. To estimate the volume under
this surface and over the region
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-region-R">
R : \{(x,y) : 0 \le x \le 2,\ 0 \le y \le 2\},
</men>

<p>
we can use the point <m>(1,1)</m> in the middle to find the height of
the blue cuboid and come up with a very bad estimate of the desired
volume.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-volume-one-box-tikz">
<caption>
A first, very rough estimate of the volume under
<m>z = x^2 + y + 5</m> over the square <m>R = [0,2]\times[0,2]</m>: a
single cuboid whose height <m>f(1,1) = 7</m> is sampled at the center
of the square, giving the estimate
<m>V \approx f(1,1)\,\Delta A = 7\cdot 4 = 28</m>.
</caption>
<image width="75%">
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space with the surface z equals x squared
plus y plus 5 drawn as a translucent blue mesh above the square with
corners zero zero and two two in the x y plane. A single translucent
blue cuboid of height seven rises from the square, and its flat top
crosses the curved surface.
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \definecolor{oigreen}{RGB}{0,158,115}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={115}{20},
      width=12.5cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=0, xmax=2.6, ymin=0, ymax=2.6, zmin=0, zmax=12.5,
      clip=false,
    ]
    % ---- back faces of the cuboid (drawn first) ----
    \fill[oiblue, opacity=0.10]
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:2,0,0)
      -- (axis cs:2,0,7) -- (axis cs:0,0,7) -- cycle;
    \fill[oiblue, opacity=0.10]
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:0,2,0)
      -- (axis cs:0,2,7) -- (axis cs:0,0,7) -- cycle;
    % ---- surface z = x^2 + y + 5 ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=faceted, faceted color=black!55,
              point meta={z},
              colormap={softz}{color=(oiblue!18!white) color=(oiblue!55!white)},
