      (axis cs:2,2,0) -- (axis cs:2,2,7)
      (axis cs:0,2,0) -- (axis cs:0,2,7)
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:0,0,7);
    % ---- sample point ----
    \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.8pt, color=oivermillion,
        forget plot] coordinates {(1,1,7)};
    \node[oivermillion, anchor=south west, font=\small]
      at (axis cs:1.05,1.05,7.15) {$f(1,1) = 7$};
    \node[anchor=south east, font=\small] at (axis cs:1,1,-1.7)
      {$(1,1)$};
    \addplot3[black!60, dashed, line width=0.6pt, samples y=0,
        forget plot] coordinates {(1,1,0) (1,1,7)};
    \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, mark size=1.4pt, color=black,
        forget plot] coordinates {(1,1,0)};
    % ---- labels ----
    \node[font=\large, anchor=north west] at (axis description cs:0.0,0.98)
      {$z = x^2 + y + 5$};
    \node[anchor=north, font=\small] at (axis cs:2.05,1.0,-0.6)
      {$R = [0,2]\times[0,2]$};
  \end{axis}
\end{tikzpicture}]]>
</latex-image>
</image>
</figure>

<p>
We can improve the estimate by splitting the region into more squares.
For instance, we can split the region into four squares to achieve a
better approximation for the desired volume, as shown in
<xref ref="fig-volume-four-boxes-tikz"/>. Similar to finding the area
under <m>f(x)</m>, if we let the number of splittings go to infinity,
our estimation becomes exact, i.e., we find the exact desired volume.
The refinement process is animated in <xref ref="fig-riemann-boxes-video"/>.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-volume-four-boxes-tikz">
<caption>
Splitting <m>R</m> into four squares improves the estimate. Each cuboid
uses the value of <m>f</m> at the center of its square:
<m>f\!\left(\tfrac12,\tfrac12\right) = 5.75</m>,
<m>f\!\left(\tfrac12,\tfrac32\right) = 6.75</m>,
<m>f\!\left(\tfrac32,\tfrac12\right) = 7.75</m>, and
<m>f\!\left(\tfrac32,\tfrac32\right) = 8.75</m>, giving
<m>V \approx (5.75 + 6.75 + 7.75 + 8.75)\cdot 1 = 29</m>.
</caption>
<image width="75%">
<description>
The same surface z equals x squared plus y plus 5 above the unit
square, now with four cuboids of different heights and colors, one
over each quarter of the square. The tops of the cuboids hug the
surface more closely than the single cuboid did.
</description>
