<p>
In the Riemann sum notation, the approximation of the volume using
<m>n</m> small rectangles is
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-riemann-sum-2d">
\text{Volume} \approx \sum_{j=1}^{m}\sum_{i=1}^{n}
f(x_i, y_j)\,\Delta A_{ij}
= \sum_{j=1}^{m}\sum_{i=1}^{n} f(x_i, y_j)\,\Delta x_i\,\Delta y_j,
</men>

<p>
and the exact volume is
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-riemann-limit-2d">
\text{Volume} = \lim_{m\to\infty}\lim_{n\to\infty}
\sum_{j=1}^{m}\sum_{i=1}^{n} f(x_i, y_j)\,\Delta x_i\,\Delta y_j.
</men>

<p>
The result of the above limit is defined as <term>the double integral
over a rectangular region</term> and is denoted as
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-double-integral-notation">
\iint_R f(x,y)\,dA
\quad\text{or}\quad
\iint_R f(x,y)\,dx\,dy.
</men>

<p>
For instance, in the example above, we have
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\text{Volume} \amp= \iint_R f(x,y)\,dA
\ \text{ or }\ \iint_R f(x,y)\,dx\,dy
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \int_0^2\!\!\int_0^2 \left(x^2 + y + 5\right)\,dx\,dy.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Next, we will study how to calculate such integrals.
</p>

<figure xml:id="fig-riemann-boxes-video">
<caption>
Animation of the Riemann-sum definition of the double integral. The
region <m>R = [0,2]\times[0,2]</m> is split into <m>1</m>, then
<m>4</m>, then <m>16</m>, then <m>64</m> squares, and a cuboid is
built over each square with height sampled at its center. The
displayed estimate approaches the exact volume
<m>\iint_R (x^2+y+5)\,dA = \frac{88}{3} \approx 29.33</m>.
</caption>
<video xml:id="video-riemann-boxes"
       source="videos/riemann_boxes_1080p.mp4"
       width="70%" aspect="16:9"/>
</figure>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-iterated-integrals">
<title>Calculating Double Integrals as Iterated Integrals</title>

<example xml:id="example-iterated-rectangle">
<title>Example I</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the double integral
<m>\displaystyle\int_0^2\!\!\int_0^2 \left(x^2 + y + 5\right)\,dx\,dy</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution>
<p>
To calculate the double integral
</p>

<men>
\int_0^2\!\!\int_0^2 \left(x^2 + y + 5\right)\,dx\,dy
</men>

<p>
we first use FTC II to integrate with respect to <m>x</m>:
</p>

<men>
\int_0^2 \left(x^2 + y + 5\right)dx
= \left.\left(\frac{x^3}{3} + xy + 5x\right)\right|_0^2
= \frac{8}{3} + 2y + 10 = 2y + \frac{38}{3},
</men>

<p>
which gives us a function of <m>y</m>, and then we integrate it with
respect to <m>y</m>:
</p>

<men>
\int_0^2 \left(2y + \frac{38}{3}\right)dy
= \left.\left(y^2 + \frac{38}{3}y\right)\right|_0^2
= 4 + \frac{76}{3} = \frac{88}{3}.
</men>
</solution>

<p>
In calculating double integrals, the following theorem can be very
useful.
</p>

<theorem xml:id="thm-fubini">
<title>Fubini's Theorem (First Form)</title>
<statement>
<p>
If <m>f(x,y)</m> is continuous throughout the rectangular region
<m>R: a \le x \le b,\ c \le y \le d</m>, then
</p>

<men xml:id="eq-fubini">
\iint_R f(x,y)\,dA
= \int_c^d\!\!\int_a^b f(x,y)\,dx\,dy
= \int_a^b\!\!\int_c^d f(x,y)\,dy\,dx.
</men>
</statement>
</theorem>

<p>
Therefore, if the integrand satisfies the conditions mentioned in the
above theorem, we can change the order of integration. For the previous
example, we can first integrate over <m>y</m>:
</p>

<men>
\int_0^2 \left(x^2 + y + 5\right)dy
= \left.\left(x^2 y + \frac{y^2}{2} + 5y\right)\right|_0^2
= 2x^2 + 12.
</men>

<p>
Then, we integrate this function of <m>x</m>:
</p>

<men>
\int_0^2 \left(2x^2 + 12\right)dx
= \left.\left(\frac{2}{3}x^3 + 12x\right)\right|_0^2
= \frac{16}{3} + 24 = \frac{88}{3},
</men>

<p>
which gives us the same result, i.e.,
<m>\displaystyle\int_0^2\!\!\int_0^2 \left(x^2+y+5\right)dx\,dy
= \frac{88}{3}</m>.
</p>

</subsection>

<!-- ================================================================ -->
<subsection xml:id="sec-iterated-integral-substitution">
<title>An Iterated Integral Requiring Substitution and Integration by Parts</title>

<example xml:id="example-iterated-substitution">
<title>Example II</title>
<statement>
<p>
Calculate the double integral
<m>\displaystyle\int_0^2\!\!\int_0^1 \frac{x}{1+xy}\,dx\,dy</m>.
</p>
</statement>
</example>

<solution>
<p>
Using <term>Fubini's theorem</term>, we first integrate over <m>y</m>,
which means that <m>x</m> is considered to be a constant. As our
<term>substitution</term>, we choose <m>u = 1 + xy</m> and
<m>du = x\,dy</m>:
</p>

<men>
\int \frac{x}{1+xy}\,dy = \int\frac{du}{u}
= \ln|u| + C = \ln|1+xy| + C,
</men>

<men>
\int_0^2 \frac{x}{1+xy}\,dy
= \Big.\ln|1+xy|\Big|_{y=0}^{y=2}
= \ln|1+2x| - \ln(1) = \ln|1+2x|.
</men>

<p>
Next, we integrate <m>\ln(1+2x)</m> with respect to <m>x</m> using the
method of <term>integration by parts</term>
<m>\left(\int u\,dv = uv - \int v\,du\right)</m>. We make the
following choices for the integration by parts:
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
u = \ln(1+2x) \amp\Rightarrow du = \frac{2}{1+2x}\,dx,
</mrow>
<mrow>
dv = dx \amp\Rightarrow v = x.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
Therefore,
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\int \ln(1+2x)\,dx
\amp= x\ln(1+2x) - \int\frac{2x}{1+2x}\,dx
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= x\ln(1+2x) - \int\frac{1+2x-1}{1+2x}\,dx
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= x\ln(1+2x) - \int\left(1 - \frac{1}{1+2x}\right)dx
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= x\ln(1+2x) - x + \frac{1}{2}\ln(1+2x) + C,
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
and hence
</p>

<md>
<mrow>
\int_0^2\!\!\int_0^1 \frac{x}{1+xy}\,dx\,dy
\amp= \int_0^1 \ln|1+2x|\,dx
</mrow>
<mrow>
\amp= \left.\left(x\ln(1+2x) - x
+ \frac{1}{2}\ln(1+2x)\right)\right|_0^1
= \frac{3}{2}\ln(3) - 1.
</mrow>
</md>

<p>
The surface <m>z = \dfrac{x}{1+xy}</m> whose volume over
<m>[0,1]\times[0,2]</m> we just computed is shown in
<xref ref="fig-substitution-surface-tikz"/>.
</p>
</solution>

<figure xml:id="fig-substitution-surface-tikz">
<caption>
The surface <m>z = \dfrac{x}{1+xy}</m> over the rectangle
<m>0 \le x \le 1</m>, <m>0 \le y \le 2</m>. The double integral of
<xref ref="example-iterated-substitution"/> is the volume between this
surface and the shaded base rectangle, and equals
<m>\frac{3}{2}\ln 3 - 1 \approx 0.648</m>.
</caption>
<image width="75%">
<description>
Three coordinate axes in space with a curved surface over the
rectangle zero to one in x and zero to two in y. The surface rises
from height zero along the y z plane to height one at the corner x
equals one, y equals zero, and slopes downward as y increases. The
base rectangle in the x y plane is shaded.
</description>
<latex-image>
<![CDATA[\begin{tikzpicture}[line join=round, line cap=round]
  % Okabe-Ito colorblind-safe palette
  \definecolor{oiblue}{RGB}{0,114,178}
  \definecolor{oivermillion}{RGB}{213,94,0}
  \begin{axis}[
      view={115}{22},
      width=12.5cm,
      axis lines=center,
      axis line style={-latex, black!75, line width=0.7pt},
      xlabel={$x$}, ylabel={$y$}, zlabel={$z$},
      label style={font=\large},
      ticks=none,
      xmin=0, xmax=1.35, ymin=0, ymax=2.6, zmin=0, zmax=1.35,
      clip=false,
    ]
    % ---- shaded base rectangle ----
    \fill[oivermillion, opacity=0.18]
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:1,0,0)
      -- (axis cs:1,2,0) -- (axis cs:0,2,0) -- cycle;
    \draw[oivermillion, line width=0.9pt]
      (axis cs:0,0,0) -- (axis cs:1,0,0) -- (axis cs:1,2,0)
      -- (axis cs:0,2,0) -- cycle;
    % ---- surface z = x/(1+xy) ----
    \addplot3[surf, shader=faceted, faceted color=black!40,
              point meta={z},
              colormap={softz}{color=(oiblue!10!white) color=(oiblue!38!white)},
              opacity=0.55, fill opacity=0.55,
              domain=0:1, samples=16,
              y domain=0:2, samples y=16,
              z buffer=sort, forget plot]
      (x, y, {x/(1+x*y)});
    % ---- labels ----
    \node[font=\large, anchor=north west] at (axis description cs:0.0,0.98)
      {$z = \dfrac{x}{1+xy}$};
    \node[oivermillion, anchor=north, font=\small]
      at (axis cs:0.75,1.3,-0.12) {$R = [0,1]\times[0,2]$};
  \end{axis}
